# ThreadSanitizer suppressions — third-party libraries only.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# The system gRPC, protobuf and abseil libraries are NOT built with
# -fsanitize=thread. TSan cannot see synchronization it was not compiled into,
# so every buffer gRPC hands between its own threads looks like an unsynchronised
# read/write. On this tree that is ~350 warnings per run of the integration
# suite, none of them in instrumentation code.
#
# The right fix is a TSan-instrumented gRPC/protobuf/abseil, which means
# building those from source under the sanitizer. That is a CI image change,
# not a code change. Until then these suppressions keep the TSan stage
# meaningful instead of permanently red.
#
# SCOPE IS DELIBERATELY NARROW. Every entry names a third-party library or
# symbol namespace. Nothing here matches instr:: code, so a genuine race in
# this repo still fails the stage -- which is the only reason to run TSan at
# all. Do not add a broad pattern here to make a failure go away; if a race
# is in our code, it is a finding.
#
# Verified: with this file the TSan stage passes; removing it returns the
# stage to ~350 warnings, all with a third-party frame in the stack.

# --- gRPC core and C++ bindings --------------------------------------------
race:libgrpc.so
race:libgrpc++.so
race:libgpr.so
race:grpc_core::
race:grpc::
# gRPC's zero-copy reader is a header, so it compiles into our binaries and
# cannot be matched by library name.
race:proto_buffer_reader.h

# --- protobuf ---------------------------------------------------------------
race:libprotobuf.so
race:google::protobuf::
# Header-inlined parser, same reason as proto_buffer_reader.h above.
race:parse_context.h

# --- abseil (pulled in by gRPC) --------------------------------------------
race:libabsl
race:absl::
